  "answers": {
    "dllRoute": "FW_RegisterCatRig from catrig_sample.dll's DllMain: redirected — the framework logged the registration \"(from a DLL)\" (none from data: the export is RigSampleCatClip, not _CatRig_), the DLL's read-back agreed (bound RigSampleCat -> RigSampleCatClip (FW_CatRigFor agrees); 1 of 1 cat rig(s) bound), and RigSampleCat showed the rig's marker over real cat parts in 3/3 shots while the vanilla control on the same parts showed none",
    "championElite": "RigSampleCat_Champion: redirected (marker in 3/3 shots), RigSampleCat_Elite: redirected (3/3) — the framework's suffix stripping gives an <Entry>_Champion / _Elite name the entry's rig. The console's spawn refuses a name that is no entry (\"No enemy named RigSampleCat_Champion\"), so the variants are declared as entries (variant_of RigSampleCat); the game's own champion path (the level spawner appends the suffix, Character::init strips it and sets the flags) hands Character::init the same string but was not driven from the harness",
    "missingLabel": "a rig with idleF/B, hitF/B and hithard/hitup/knockback F/B only: hit-drawn (poses {\"idle\":23,\"hit\":2,\"other\":0,\"none\":0}: the two non-lethal hits showed our hitF/B pose); the killing hit (death labels) and every other label the rig lacks were silent no-ops — the cat kept its idle frame, no crash, no line in debug.log or mewframework.log, and the fight ran exactly like the vanilla control (rig HP per dump 15 10 5 0 0 0 0 0 0, rounds 1 -> 5; control HP 15 10 5 0 0 0 0 0 0, rounds 1 -> 5). Visible difference: at 0 HP the vanilla cat lies dead on its tile while the rig cat keeps STANDING in its idle pose (no death label) for the rest of the run"
  }
}
